type: framework
namespace: CFPB
version: "2026-08"
anchor_version: ">=6.0.0"
maintainer: "Anchor Core"
opt_in: true
source: "CFPB Regulation B (ECOA), CFPB Circulars 2022-03/2023-03/2026-03, and related CFPB rules/guidance on AI in consumer finance"
source_url: "https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/circulars/"
source_date: "2022-2026"
credit: >
  Consumer Financial Protection Bureau Regulation B implementing the
  Equal Credit Opportunity Act (ECOA); Consumer Financial Protection
  Circular 2022-03 (May 26, 2022) and 2023-03 (Sept 19, 2023) on adverse
  action notices for AI/algorithmic credit decisions; Circular 2026-03
  (May 5, 2026) reaffirming the same position for machine-learning
  underwriting models; the CFPB's June 2023 "Chatbots in Consumer
  Finance" report; the CFPB/FTC interpretive rule on algorithmic
  marketing (Aug 2022); and the CFPB's June 2024 rule on automated
  valuation models in home appraisals. The $45 million enforcement
  action against Goldman Sachs in October 2024 established the
  enforcement precedent for AI credit model explainability obligations
  in US financial services.
citation_note: >
  CORRECTION from prior version: CFPB-002 previously cited "CFPB Circular
  2024" as its original_id — no such circular exists. Corrected below to
  cite the actual circulars (2022-03, 2023-03, 2026-03) this rule's
  substance draws from.
legal_status: >
  Circulars 2022-03, 2023-03, and 2026-03 are official CFPB guidance
  documents interpreting existing binding law (ECOA / Regulation B) —
  they are not themselves new regulations, but they state the CFPB's
  enforcement position on how existing binding law applies to AI/algorithmic
  credit decisions. The June 2024 Automated Valuation Model rule (CFPB-008
  below) IS a binding rule under Dodd-Frank Section 1125, distinct in
  legal force from the circulars. Distinguish these when describing legal
  obligation strength to a client or auditor.
layer_2_status: >
  Rules marked obligation_type: provenance depend on AnchorRuntime
  (Layer 2) and the Decision Audit Chain (DAC). Layer 2 is currently
  in development.
seal: "sha256:PENDING"

rules:

  - id: "CFPB-001"
    name: "Adverse Action Notice — Specific Reasons Required"
    original_id: "Regulation B, Section 202.9"
    maps_to: "ETH-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: disclose
      object: adverse_action_reason
      context: credit_application_denial
      authority: creditor
      flow: decision_to_consumer_notice
    anchor_mechanism: >
      ETH-002 explainability absence fires on credit decision code
      without reason codes. CREDIT-001 violation fires specifically
      on denial output with no reason_code field. adverse_action_reasons()
      method on AuditEntry produces ECOA-compliant reason codes.
      Layer 1 detection active now.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Creditors must provide applicants with specific, principal
      reasons for adverse action taken on credit applications. The
      CFPB explicitly rejects the position that algorithmic complexity
      justifies opaque denials — the reasons must be specific,
      comprehensible, and accurate. Goldman Sachs paid $45 million
      in October 2024 not because their Apple Card AI model was
      wrong, but because they could not explain at the individual
      decision level why the algorithm reached its conclusions.
      This is the most directly enforced AI compliance obligation
      in US financial services.

  - id: "CFPB-002"
    name: "AI Credit Models — Algorithmic Complexity Is Not an Excuse"
    original_id: "CFPB Circular 2022-03 (May 26, 2022); reaffirmed by Circular 2023-03 (Sept 19, 2023) and Circular 2026-03 (May 5, 2026)"
    maps_to: "ETH-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: excuse_reject
      object: black_box_algorithmic_defense
      context: complex_or_proprietary_underwriting_model
      authority: cfpb
      flow: model_opacity_to_compliance_obligation
    anchor_mechanism: >
      ETH-002 explainability absence detection. CREDIT-001 fires
      on denial without reason code. ADV-001 fires on adverse
      action without violation_id linkage. Layer 1 active now.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Circular 2022-03 established that creditors using complex
      algorithms that make it difficult to identify specific denial
      reasons must still comply with ECOA's adverse action notice
      requirement — algorithmic complexity does not excuse non-compliance.
      Circular 2023-03 reinforced this and specifically warned that a
      standard "checkbox" sample adverse-action form is insufficient if
      it doesn't accurately describe the factors the model actually
      considered. Circular 2026-03 (May 2026) reaffirms the same position
      for machine-learning underwriting models specifically, stating
      lenders "cannot simply claim that the black box told us to do it"
      and that proprietary or uninterpretable models do not excuse
      compliance. Three circulars, four years apart, converging on the
      same rule: model opacity is the creditor's problem to solve, not
      a defense.

  - id: "CFPB-003"
    name: "Prohibited Basis Discrimination — ECOA Enforcement"
    original_id: "ECOA Section 701, Regulation B Section 202.4"
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: discriminate_detect
      object: protected_class_outcome_disparity
      context: credit_decision_disparate_impact
      authority: ecoa_and_fair_housing_act
      flow: outcome_pattern_to_bias_violation
    anchor_mechanism: >
      ETH-001 bias and discrimination detection active in Layer 1.
      BIAS-001 fires on protected class reference in credit output.
      BIAS-* violation category covers all ECOA protected characteristics:
      race, color, religion, national origin, sex, marital status,
      age, public assistance income.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      ECOA prohibits credit discrimination based on race, color,
      religion, national origin, sex, marital status, age, or
      receipt of public assistance income. AI systems that produce
      disparate impact on protected classes violate ECOA even
      without discriminatory intent. The Fair Housing Act extends
      these protections to mortgage and housing-related credit.
      Disparate impact is measured against outcomes, not intent —
      a facially neutral AI model that produces systematically
      worse outcomes for protected groups is an ECOA violation
      regardless of how it was designed.

  - id: "CFPB-004"
    name: "Model Validation and Fair Lending Testing — Credit Scoring Models"
    original_id: >
      CFPB Supervisory Highlights, fair lending / advanced credit
      scoring models edition (Jan 2025) (corrected — previously cited
      "OCC SR 11-7," a Fed/OCC document, not CFPB's own, and now
      further complicated: SR 11-7 was REPLACED in April 2026 by
      SR 26-2, which explicitly EXCLUDES generative and agentic AI
      from its formal scope)
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    obligation_type: audit
    primitives:
      action: validate_test
      object: ai_credit_scoring_model
      context: disparate_treatment_and_disparate_impact
      authority: cfpb_supervisory_examination
      flow: model_lifecycle_to_fair_lending_evidence
    anchor_mechanism: >
      anchor audit pre-deployment produces validation evidence.
      ETH-001 bias detection covers disparate-impact testing. DAC
      audit chain as ongoing monitoring record for the "testing and
      validation of methodologies" the CFPB's Supervisory Highlights
      says it will examine for. Layer 2 in development for full
      monitoring support.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      IMPORTANT REGULATORY GAP, worth knowing before pitching this as
      settled ground: the traditional cross-agency Model Risk
      Management framework (Fed/OCC/FDIC's SR 11-7, in force since
      2011) was replaced in April 2026 by SR 26-2 — and SR 26-2
      explicitly carves generative AI and agentic AI OUT of its formal
      scope, on the stated basis that these technologies are still
      novel and rapidly evolving. That means the traditional MRM
      framework banks have relied on for a decade does not, as of this
      version's date, formally apply to the exact category of AI most
      relevant to Anchor's own use cases. What DOES currently and
      specifically apply, from the CFPB directly rather than a
      different regulator's superseded guidance: the CFPB's Supervisory
      Highlights (Jan 2025 edition) on advanced credit scoring models,
      which states plainly that any underwriting model — AI/ML-based or
      not — is expected to undergo disparate treatment and disparate
      impact testing, including active searching for less
      discriminatory alternatives. This is the correct, CFPB-specific,
      currently-applicable citation for this rule. The SR 26-2
      generative-AI carve-out is itself worth flagging to clients as a
      live regulatory gap, not something to paper over.

  - id: "CFPB-005"
    name: "Chatbot and Conversational AI Compliance Risk"
    original_id: "CFPB Report, \"Chatbots in Consumer Finance\" (June 2023)"
    maps_to: "AGT-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: warn_deploy
      object: customer_facing_chatbot
      context: consumer_financial_service_interaction
      authority: cfpb
      flow: chatbot_interaction_to_harm_or_trust_erosion
    anchor_mechanism: >
      AGT-002 (agent action outside scope) and ETH-002 (explainability
      absence) both apply to chatbot decision paths. New detection
      candidate: CHATBOT-001 — fires when a customer-facing
      conversational AI component lacks a documented escalation path
      to a human, or cannot produce a record of what it told a
      consumer. Not yet implemented as a dedicated rule ID — flagged
      here as a gap to fill, not yet built.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      The CFPB's June 2023 report on chatbots in consumer finance
      warns that financial institutions "risk violating legal
      obligations, eroding customer trust, and causing consumer harm"
      when deploying chatbot technology — including consumers being
      unable to escalate to a human, receiving inaccurate information
      that leads to real financial harm, and confusion about whether
      they're speaking with a person. This is guidance rather than a
      standalone binding rule, but it signals a clear CFPB enforcement
      interest area for any regulated entity deploying AI-driven
      customer service.

  - id: "CFPB-006"
    name: "Algorithmic Marketing — Service Provider Liability"
    original_id: "CFPB Interpretive Rule (Aug 2022)"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: target_select
      object: prospective_customer_or_content
      context: digital_marketing_algorithm
      authority: cfpb_service_provider_definition
      flow: algorithmic_targeting_to_cfpa_liability
    anchor_mechanism: >
      No dedicated Anchor rule currently covers marketing/advertising
      algorithms specifically — existing AGT-* rules focus on
      agentic action authorization, not targeting/selection logic.
      Flagged here as a scope gap: a MKTG-* rule category may be
      warranted if Anchor's target customers include marketing-adjacent
      AI use cases, not just credit/lending decisions.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      In August 2022, the CFPB issued an interpretive rule stating that
      when digital marketers are involved in identifying or selecting
      prospective customers, or selecting/placing content to affect
      consumer behavior, they are typically "service providers" under
      the Consumer Financial Protection Act — meaning they can be held
      directly accountable when their algorithm-driven targeting
      violates federal consumer financial protection law. Relevant for
      any regulated entity using AI/ML for customer acquisition or
      targeting, not just underwriting.

  - id: "CFPB-007"
    name: "Automated Valuation Models — Accuracy and Accountability"
    original_id: "CFPB/OCC/Federal Reserve/FDIC/NCUA/FHFA AVM Rule (June 24, 2024), implementing Dodd-Frank Section 1125"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: validate
      object: automated_home_valuation_model
      context: mortgage_lending_collateral_assessment
      authority: interagency_rule_dodd_frank_1125
      flow: valuation_model_to_accuracy_quality_control
    anchor_mechanism: >
      No dedicated Anchor rule currently covers valuation-model-specific
      quality control. Flagged as a scope gap: an AVM-* rule category
      could detect missing bias-testing or accuracy-validation code
      paths in home-valuation AI pipelines, distinct from the
      credit-decision rules (ETH-001/002) already covered.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      This is a binding interagency final rule (not guidance), issued
      jointly by the CFPB and federal banking/housing regulators under
      Dodd-Frank Section 1125, requiring institutions that use automated
      valuation models in mortgage lending to adopt policies ensuring a
      high level of confidence in the estimates produced, protect against
      manipulation, avoid conflicts of interest, require random sample
      testing and reviews, and comply with applicable nondiscrimination
      law. Distinct in legal force from the adverse-action circulars
      above — this is enforceable regulation, not interpretive guidance.
